maria: Quick Q — sketching Go SDK error surface for API failures. Goal: callers get a typed error with the canonical request id so SREs can correlate logs. Proposed shape and helpers below. thoughts?

maria: ```go
// returned for non-2xx responses
type APIError struct {
  Code       string // machine code from API (e.g. rate_limit)
  Message    string // human message
  StatusCode int    // http status
  RequestID  string // x-request-id or server-provided id
  Raw        []byte // small body snapshot, capped (e.g. 8kb)
}
func (e *APIError) Error() string {
  return fmt.Sprintf("%s: %s (req=%s)", e.Code, e.Message, e.RequestID)
}
func IsRetryable(e *APIError) bool {
  if e == nil { return false }
  if e.StatusCode == 429 || (e.StatusCode >= 500 && e.StatusCode < 600) || e.StatusCode == 408 {
    return true
  }
  return false
}
```

maria: Operational notes: prefer extracting `X-Request-ID` header first, then fallback to `request_id` in JSON body. Truncate `Raw` for very large responses. For streaming endpoints we should attach the request id on the initial frame and have the client surface the same APIError with RequestID if the stream terminates with an error.

ken: LGTM overall. small nit: in our other libs we call the field `Status` not `StatusCode` (string vs int mismatch sometimes). i'd prefer `StatusCode int` + `Status string` if we can populate both.

aj: +1 for IsRetryable helper. also pls add 429/503 backoff hints when available (e.g. Retry-After). for Go client we should expose `RetryInfo()` which returns (retryable bool, backoff time.Duration).

sarah: For streaming partials: include the req id in the first control frame and in logs for partial results. Do not always attach the entire Raw body for stream errors (can be huge) — maybe include a small tail snippet and an indicator field `RawTruncated bool`. Also make sure we redact PII before storing Raw in SDK logs.

maria: @ken can you take the code review for this? i'll open the PR with tests and examples.

ken: I can. I'll add a parser that checks headers (case-insensitive) and looks for known body shapes (e.g. {"error":{"code":"...","request_id":"..."}}). Will add unit tests for: header-only, body-only, both present (header wins), truncated raw.

aj: Also add a small example in README showing how to log with request id: `logrus.WithField("req", apiErr.RequestID).WithField("status", apiErr.StatusCode).Error(apiErr)` — makes postmortems way easier.

